  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "About the Mongolian script",
      "body": "<p>The Mongols adopted this script in the early 13th century, under Genghis Khan, from the Old Uyghur alphabet, which itself came from Sogdian and ultimately from Aramaic. It is one of the few scripts written in vertical columns that run from left to right.</p>\n<p>Mongolia switched to Cyrillic in the 1940s, but the traditional script is still the main script for Mongolian in Inner Mongolia, China, and Mongolia has been bringing it back into schools and official documents.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Letters change with their place",
      "body": "<p>Like Arabic, Mongolian is joined: letters hang from a central stem and take different shapes at the start, middle and end of a word. Several letters that are distinct on their own look the same in the middle of a word (o and u, ö and ü, and sometimes t and d), and readers tell them apart from the word.</p>\n<p>This app teaches the letters on their own first. Once you know them, look at whole words such as <span lang=\"mn-Mong\">ᠮᠣᠩᠭᠣᠯ</span> (Mongol) to see the joined forms.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Spelling and speech",
      "body": "<p>The spelling reflects how Mongolian sounded centuries ago. Modern speech often drops the last vowel and shortens others: <span lang=\"mn-Mong\">ᠰᠠᠷᠠ</span> is spelled sara but said sar; <span lang=\"mn-Mong\">ᠤᠰᠤ</span> is spelled usu and said us. The recordings here follow modern speech; the transliteration follows the spelling.</p>"
    }
  ]
